=== ML-DSA hint experiment: does LAS compose with FIPS 204 as specified? ===
ML-DSA-65  (K=6, L=5, TAU=49, GAMMA1=524288, GAMMA2=261888, OMEGA=55, ETA=4)
hint vector, Power2Round and high/low-bit decomposition are ALL ENABLED.
iterations per variant: 200

wire sizes: ML-DSA signature 3309 B | public key 1952 B | statement Y 4416 B
  (the statement is a full R_q^K element: Power2Round compresses the public
   key, but it cannot compress Y, which enters the verification identity
   before any rounding.)

control: unmodified ML-DSA sign -> verify : OK
fidelity gate: mirrored ML-DSA sign (no statement) -> stock verify : 200 / 200
baseline attempts/Sign (matched, same loop)  : 5.570

--- VARIANT 0 -- the naive port ---
  ML-DSA's signing path verbatim, with exactly ONE change: the challenge is
  hashed over HighBits(w + Y) instead of HighBits(w). The committed high bits,
  the low-bits rejection test and MakeHint are all still taken around w.
  This is what treating the hint machinery as a black box produces.

  P1 PreVerify accepts pre-signature                        0 / 200     FAILS ALWAYS
  P2 stock Verify REJECTS pre-signature (tripwire)        200 / 200     HOLDS
  P3 Adapt succeeds                                         0 / 200     FAILS ALWAYS
  P4 stock Verify ACCEPTS adapted signature  <-- decisive      0 / 200     FAILS ALWAYS
  P5 Ext recovers the exact witness                         0 / 200     FAILS ALWAYS
  P6 adapted ||z||inf < GAMMA1-BETA                         0 / 200     FAILS ALWAYS
  P7 hint weight <= OMEGA                                 200 / 200     HOLDS

  rejection-sampling attempts/PreSign : 4.585
  hint weight mean / max / OMEGA      : 37.2 / 55 / 55

--- VARIANT 1 -- the commitment path moved onto w + Y ---
  The signer knows Y at PreSign time, so the ENTIRE commitment path is taken
  around w + Y: the committed high bits, the low-bits rejection test and
  MakeHint. PreSign also rejects at GAMMA1 - BETA - ETA so the adapted z still
  clears ML-DSA's own bound. This is the most faithful repair available
  without weakening ML-DSA or handing the adapter a signing key.

  P1 PreVerify accepts pre-signature                      200 / 200     HOLDS
  P2 stock Verify REJECTS pre-signature (tripwire)        200 / 200     HOLDS
  P3 Adapt succeeds                                       200 / 200     HOLDS
  P4 stock Verify ACCEPTS adapted signature  <-- decisive    200 / 200     HOLDS
  P5 Ext recovers the exact witness                       200 / 200     HOLDS
  P6 adapted ||z||inf < GAMMA1-BETA                       200 / 200     HOLDS
  P7 hint weight <= OMEGA                                 200 / 200     HOLDS

  rejection-sampling attempts/PreSign : 5.135
  hint weight mean / max / OMEGA      : 37.9 / 53 / 55

=== Cost of the adaptor layer on ML-DSA (matched baseline) ===
  attempts/PreSign vs attempts/Sign : 5.135 vs 5.570  (-7.8%)
  statement Y on the wire           : 4416 B, against a 3309 B signature
  (Y is larger than the signature it accompanies: Power2Round shrinks the
   public key to t1, but Y enters the identity before any rounding.)

=== What this shows ===
Read the P4 row of each variant. P4 is the claim 'the adapted signature is a
fully ordinary ML-DSA signature', i.e. what an unchanged consensus verifier
would have to accept.

Structural findings that hold regardless of the counts above, and that the
code makes checkable:
  (a) PreSign CANNOT be ML-DSA's Sign. Sign commits to HighBits(w), which
      contains no statement; any adaptor needs the statement inside the hash,
      so the signing algorithm is modified by construction.
  (b) PreVerify CANNOT be ML-DSA's Verify. It has to add Y before UseHint
      (mldsa_las.c, mldsa_las_preverify), and the stock verifier has no Y.
      A deployed verifier can therefore check adapted signatures but never
      pre-signatures.
  (c) Adapt CANNOT repair a hint. MakeHint needs c*t0 and w - c*s2, both
      derived from the SIGNER's secret key; the adapting party holds only the
      witness. Whatever hint PreSign wrote is what the adapted signature
      carries.
  (d) The statement is not compressible the way the public key is. t is sent
      as t1 via Power2Round; Y enters the identity before rounding and is
      sent in full.

These are the modifications the simplified scheme avoids by disabling the
hint, Power2Round and the high/low-bit split -- which is the design decision
this experiment was built to justify or refute.
